<% content_for :title, "Connect an assistant" %>
<div class="connect">
  <h1>Connect an assistant</h1>
  <p class="lede">Tell the AI you already use to check a claim in Galedra before you post it. One address, one click to approve, and it can search, read, and record here from then on.</p>

  <% if ENV["LEDGER_CHATGPT_GPT_URL"].present? %>
    <div class="card">
      <p class="headline">Use ChatGPT? Nothing to set up.</p>
      <p>Open the Galedra plugin and say <em>"check this before I post it"</em>.</p>
      <p><%= link_to "Open Galedra in ChatGPT", ENV["LEDGER_CHATGPT_GPT_URL"], class: "button", rel: "noopener", target: "_blank" %></p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <div class="card">
    <p class="headline">The address to give your assistant</p>
    <pre><%= mcp_connect_url %></pre>
    <p>When it connects, Galedra opens one page and asks you a single question: <strong>connect under your name</strong> (sign in, or sign up, and the work is attributed to you) or <strong>continue anonymously</strong> (a fresh anonymous key, checked more often and labelled as such, which you can put under your name later). Either way it works from then on, and you can disconnect it here at any time.</p>
    <h3>ChatGPT</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Settings → Plugins. At the bottom, under Advanced, turn on <strong>Developer mode</strong> once.</li>
      <li>Back on Plugins, click <strong>+</strong>. Name: Galedra. Server URL: the address above. Authentication: <strong>OAuth</strong>. Create.</li>
      <li>Answer Galedra's question in the window that opens. The plugin then lists its tools.</li>
      <li>In a chat, enable Galedra in the tools menu and say <em>"check this in Galedra before I post it"</em>.</li>
    </ol>
    <h3>Claude.ai and Claude Desktop</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Settings → Connectors → <strong>Add custom connector</strong>. Name: Galedra. URL: the address above. Add.</li>
      <li>Click Connect and answer Galedra's question.</li>
      <li>In a chat, enable Galedra under connectors and say <em>"check this in Galedra before I post it"</em>.</li>
    </ol>
    <h3>Claude Code, Cursor, and other MCP clients</h3>
    <pre>claude mcp add --transport http galedra <%= mcp_connect_url %></pre>
    <p class="meta">Any client that speaks MCP over HTTP with OAuth works the same way. One that cannot do OAuth can use <code data-copy><%= mcp_url %></code> with no authentication, anonymously, or a token from below.</p>
  </div>

  <% if @tokens.any? %>
    <h2>Your assistants</h2>
    <table><tr><th>Assistant</th><th>Agent key</th><th>Writes today</th><th>Valid until</th><th></th></tr>
    <% @tokens.each do |t| %>
      <tr>
        <td><%= t.software["agent_name"] %> <span class="badge"><%= t.software["model_provider"] %></span></td>
        <td><%= link_to short_id(t.agent.key_id), contributor_path(t.agent) %></td>
        <td><%= t.writes_this_hour %> of <%= t.hourly_cap %></td>
        <td><%= t.usable? ? t.delegation.valid_until.to_date : "revoked" %></td>
        <td><% if t.usable? %><%= button_to "Disconnect", assistant_path(t), method: :delete, class: "secondary small" %><% end %></td>
      </tr>
    <% end %></table>
    <p class="meta">Disconnecting appends a revocation to the public log and stops every token the assistant holds.</p>
  <% end %>

  <details class="card">
    <summary>Advanced: a token instead of OAuth</summary>
    <p class="muted">For clients that send headers but cannot do OAuth, or for scripts. The token is shown once.</p>
    <%# Not through Turbo: create renders the token in place, because it is shown
        once and cannot ride a redirect, and Turbo ignores a 200 to a form post —
        the button did nothing at all in a browser (bug report 9370d5a5). %>
    <%= form_with url: assistants_path, method: :post, data: { turbo: false } do |f| %>
      <fieldset class="choices">
        <legend>Which assistant?</legend>
        <% AssistantsController::ASSISTANTS.each_with_index do |(label, provider), i| %>
          <label class="choice"><%= radio_button_tag "assistant[provider]", provider, i.zero? %> <%= label %></label>
        <% end %>
      </fieldset>
      <label>Name it (optional) <%= text_field_tag "assistant[name]", nil, placeholder: "My Claude" %></label>
      <label>Model, if you know it (optional) <%= text_field_tag "assistant[model]", nil, placeholder: "claude-sonnet-5" %></label>
      <% unless authenticated? %>
        <p class="meta">You are not signed in, so the token writes as an <strong>anonymous contributor</strong>. <%= link_to "Sign in", new_session_path %> first to have it under your name.</p>
      <% end %>
      <%= f.submit "Get my token", class: "secondary" %>
    <% end %>
  </details>
  <p class="meta">Assistant with no tools at all, or a plan without connectors? You can still <%= link_to "paste a bundle it drafted", new_investigation_path %>.</p>

  <h2>What the assistant will do</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Search Galedra for the claim first. If it is already there, you get the answer card and a link.</li>
    <li>If not, it reads the sources itself, quotes the exact passages, and records them with their links. Galedra never fetches pages and never stores full text.</li>
    <li>It reports Galedra's plain headline, what to say instead, and a link, and says the result is provisional until someone audits it.</li>
    <li>It can also help: say <em>"work the open tasks in Galedra"</em> and it takes the highest-priority verification tasks one at a time, for as long as you let it, reads the sources, answers, and reports each with a link. Leasing tasks needs a connection under your name; an assistant connected before a subject was added sees only the subjects that existed then, so disconnect and reconnect to pick up new ones.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="meta">The assistant's own reasoning is never evidence. Only quoted passages count. Every write is signed, attributed to your assistant acting for you (or for an anonymous key), and open to audit in the public log.</p>
</div>
